<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-French Convention of 1898</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Niger Convention</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-French Convention of 1898 was an agreement signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 14 June 1898 that settled the frontier between British and French territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, especially along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. It assigned Bussa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>, and Nikki to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which became part of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-french-convention-of-1898.jpg" alt="Anglo-French Convention of 1898" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_de_l%27Afrique_d%27apr%C3%A8s_la_convention_franco-anglaise_-_btv1b10112108w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
